{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw9nNFAyR5hUSUmgDUti","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R43mDjE3fjcrGi1R2ENy57","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\nrmse}{\\mathrm{nRMSE}}\n\n\\title{How Far Do $k$ Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nEvaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair reduces pooled normalized RMSE by 43.4\\% relative to column-mean imputation (nRMSE 0.562) and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its edge over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 1.6\\%. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n\nPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n\n\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n\n\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\nLet $X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times N}$ be a history matrix with observation mask $O\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M\\times N}$; benchmark $j$ has history mean $m_j$ and standard deviation $\\sigma_j$ computed over its observed cells. A new model $i$ has scores $y_{ij}$ on a subset $\\mathcal{O}_i$ of benchmarks (not every model was run on every benchmark). A method must (a) output a set $S$ of $k$ benchmark indices \\emph{before} seeing any new-model score (non-adaptive selection), and (b) given $y_{iS}$ (only the entries in $\\mathcal{O}_i\\cap S$), output predictions $\\hat y_{ij}$ for all $j$. Scoring uses the cells $\\mathcal{C}_i=\\mathcal{O}_i\\setminus S$. For one evaluation episode with $T$ held-out models,\n\\begin{equation}\n\\nrmse=\\sqrt{\\frac1T\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{C}_i|}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{C}_i}\\Big(\\frac{\\hat y_{ij}-y_{ij}}{\\sigma_j}\\Big)^2},\n\\end{equation}\nso every held-out model has equal weight and every benchmark is on the scale of its history spread. A baseline $b$ yields the skill\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathrm{skill}_b = \\mathrm{clip}\\big(1-\\nrmse/\\nrmse_b,\\,-1,\\,1\\big),\n\\end{equation}\nthe relative error reduction against that baseline, which is $0$ at parity and negative when worse.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\nIf benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. We state this only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:\n(1) a per-benchmark rank-Gauss transform makes marginals closer to normal, with predictions mapped back through the stored history quantiles and clipped to the observed history range;\n(2) a wrapper selector scores candidate benchmarks directly by cross-validated held-out-model error, instead of the model-based trace alone;\n(3) a per-target non-negative stack lets each target benchmark weight several imputers (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, nearest-neighbour models, column median), with a median-shrinkage fallback when the stack does not beat the median in cross-validation.\nAll tuning uses history models only.\n\n\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n\\textbf{Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation).} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n\n\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n\\paragraph{Representation and EM.} Two representations of the history are built: z-scoring (clipped at $\\pm8$) and an empirical rank-Gauss map $\\Phi^{-1}\\big((r-\\tfrac12)/n\\big)$ per benchmark (average ranks; benchmarks with fewer than eight observations or four distinct values fall back to z-scores). For each, EM fits $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ with missing entries, applying diagonal shrinkage $\\Sigma\\leftarrow(1-\\epsilon)\\Sigma+\\epsilon\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Sigma)$ ($\\epsilon=0.02$) in each M-step (at most 60 iterations, tolerance $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget).\n\n\\paragraph{Selection.} Candidate subsets come from (i) covariance-trace greedy A-optimal selection: with $R$ the current posterior covariance, add $j$ maximising $\\sum_{i\\in U,i\\ne j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$, then update $R\\leftarrow R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$; and (ii) a greedy forward \\emph{wrapper} that adds the benchmark minimising five-fold held-out-model squared error of the conditional-Gaussian predictor, with all candidates scored by batched matrix products and an optional coverage weighting (benchmarks missing for many history models are less informative). For each representation and candidate rule, the subset with the lowest history cross-validation error in raw benchmark units is chosen (up to 250 candidates per step).\n\n\\paragraph{Imputation.} For a new model, the Gaussian conditional mean $\\mu+\\Sigma_{\\cdot S}(\\Sigma_{SS}+\\lambda I)^{-1}(y_S-\\mu_S)$ conditions only on the selected benchmarks that model actually has. Shrinkage, factor rank and $\\lambda$ are tuned by five-fold history cross-validation. The stack uses, per target benchmark, non-negative weights over six base predictors (three Gaussian conditional means at shrinkage 0, 0.15 and 0.3, ridge on the selected benchmarks, and kNN with 5 and 15 neighbours) plus an implicit column-median term, fitted on out-of-fold history predictions and regularised toward a pooled weight vector; a regularisation strength is chosen from six values by cross-validation. Outputs are mapped back to raw units, clipped to the history range, and replaced by the observed value for selected benchmarks; a model with no selected score receives the column median.\n\n\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds per seed. The method presumes a history matrix with enough coverage per benchmark and a new model whose scores are exchangeable with history models after transformation, which family-disjoint evaluation tests but does not guarantee. Some stages are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\n\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation.} Within a leaderboard, values are averaged over seeds, then over its $k$ grid with equal weights; the pooled metric averages the three leaderboards with weight $1/3$ each. The permuted-control gap is the control $\\nrmse$ minus the matched real-episode $\\nrmse$, averaged over seeds and then over leaderboards. A run counts as valid only if all episodes complete with finite predictions, real episodes beat their controls, and pooled skill against column mean and random-$k$ ridge is positive. The sealed evaluation finished with outcome \\emph{qualified}. This statement concerns the evaluated setting; no further qualification diagnostics were retained.\n\n\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_results.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Main results}\nPooled over the three leaderboards, the proposed pair reaches $\\nrmse=0.562$ and a skill of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, that is a 43.4\\% reduction in relative error (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]]\nThe three per-seed pooled skills are 0.433, 0.427 and 0.433, so the stochasticity of the method's own cross-validation folds moves the headline by less than 0.006. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\nThis supports H1 in the evaluated setting. Because all three seeds use identical held-out models, this spread does not measure how the result would change with different held-out models, and we report no confidence interval for the pooled values.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics of the frozen pair (mean over seeds 201--203; hierarchy described in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}). Lower $\\nrmse$ is better; skill is relative error reduction against the named baseline.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\n\\midrule\nPooled $\\nrmse$ & 0.562 & mean over 3 leaderboards\\\\\n\\quad MTEB / HELM Lite / Open LLM LB & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per-leaderboard $\\nrmse$\\\\\n\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787 / 0.722 / 0.664 / 0.638 & error vs.\\ budget\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ column mean & 0.434 & H1\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ greedy-forward ridge & 0.099 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ random-$k$ ridge & 0.016 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nPermuted-control gap (nRMSE) & 0.678 & control minus real\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_helm]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_oll]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\nOn MTEB, $\\nrmse$ falls monotonically from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.722 at $k{=}5$, 0.664 at $k{=}10$ and 0.638 at $k{=}20$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}a). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20]]\nThe mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n\\paragraph{Control.} The permuted-observation control raises $\\nrmse$ by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes (Figure~\\ref{tab:final} lists the value). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\nHence the predictions depend on the new model's own scores on the selected benchmarks rather than on a marginal prior alone. The gap is an absolute $\\nrmse$ difference and should be read against the pooled error of 0.562.\n\n\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has about 1.6\\% lower relative error than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\nBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n\n\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n\n\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\nDuring development, three configurations were scored on a separate MTEB partition (disjoint groups, 280 columns, 131 history and 32 held-out models) with two seeds and $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$. Table~\\ref{tab:search} keeps these numbers apart from the sealed ones; $\\nrmse$ values are not comparable across the two partitions.\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition comparison (MTEB, two seeds). Score is the mean skill against column mean. The seed columns give the two per-seed scores.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11 / 12 & $\\nrmse$ & Skill vs.\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\ gap\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair (trace greedy + conditional mean) & 0.220 & 0.220 / 0.220 & 1.067 & 0.100 & 0.522\\\\\nProposed pair (wrapper + stack) & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\nProposed + latent-skill member & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on the best-scoring configuration; the exact tie means the retained scores alone cannot distinguish the two sources (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with 43.4\\% lower pooled relative error than column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within 1.6\\% of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked pair on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562, a 43.4\\% relative reduction against column means, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\nGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Columns & History models & Held-out models & Groups (hist./held-out) & $k$ grid\\\\\n\\midrule\nMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\nOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\nHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (\\texttt{src/program.py}, functions \\texttt{em\\_gaussian\\_covariance}, \\texttt{greedy\\_select\\_conditional\\_variance}, \\texttt{wrapper\\_forward\\_select\\_cv}, \\texttt{stack\\_predictors\\_nnls}, \\texttt{predict\\_raw}). % @evidence[[results/ideas/02_wrapper_cv_error_benchmark_selection_with_robust_quantile_transformed_stacked_imputation/program_1/src/program.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair.}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\n\\midrule\nEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\nCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\nCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\nFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\nRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\nSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\nWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\nStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\nStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\nClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\nWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\nThe latent code is therefore executed but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated program tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The description in the main text is of the shared stack, and the extension is described here for completeness. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n\n\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\nProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\nProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\nThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\nEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used for research ideation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their records.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":160326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2706},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29bb2fd2-b870-449b-bdba-4ce244096eb3","uuid":"17223af6-dde1-4a74-a51a-ecf66fd8a478","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:38:49.191Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw9nM9wNj76unisjUB21"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R43mDjE3fjcrGi1R2ENy57","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /workspace/paper/paper.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29bb2fd2-b870-449b-bdba-4ce244096eb3","uuid":"4204e83a-3a8e-4adb-b825-80238726e7cf","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:38:49.218Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\nrmse}{\\mathrm{nRMSE}}\n\n\\title{How Far Do $k$ Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nEvaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair reduces pooled normalized RMSE by 43.4\\% relative to column-mean imputation (nRMSE 0.562) and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its edge over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 1.6\\%. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n\nPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n\n\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n\n\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\nLet $X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times N}$ be a history matrix with observation mask $O\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M\\times N}$; benchmark $j$ has history mean $m_j$ and standard deviation $\\sigma_j$ computed over its observed cells. A new model $i$ has scores $y_{ij}$ on a subset $\\mathcal{O}_i$ of benchmarks (not every model was run on every benchmark). A method must (a) output a set $S$ of $k$ benchmark indices \\emph{before} seeing any new-model score (non-adaptive selection), and (b) given $y_{iS}$ (only the entries in $\\mathcal{O}_i\\cap S$), output predictions $\\hat y_{ij}$ for all $j$. Scoring uses the cells $\\mathcal{C}_i=\\mathcal{O}_i\\setminus S$. For one evaluation episode with $T$ held-out models,\n\\begin{equation}\n\\nrmse=\\sqrt{\\frac1T\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{C}_i|}\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{C}_i}\\Big(\\frac{\\hat y_{ij}-y_{ij}}{\\sigma_j}\\Big)^2},\n\\end{equation}\nso every held-out model has equal weight and every benchmark is on the scale of its history spread. A baseline $b$ yields the skill\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathrm{skill}_b = \\mathrm{clip}\\big(1-\\nrmse/\\nrmse_b,\\,-1,\\,1\\big),\n\\end{equation}\nthe relative error reduction against that baseline, which is $0$ at parity and negative when worse.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\nIf benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. Choosing $S$ greedily to reduce this trace (an A-optimal design) is therefore a principled selector \\emph{under the Gaussian model}. We state this only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment, and real score matrices are bounded, skewed and sparsely observed, which can make the trace criterion misaligned with realised held-out error. Three design choices address this misalignment:\n(1) a per-benchmark rank-Gauss transform makes marginals closer to normal, with predictions mapped back through the stored history quantiles and clipped to the observed history range;\n(2) a wrapper selector scores candidate benchmarks directly by cross-validated held-out-model error, instead of the model-based trace alone;\n(3) a per-target non-negative stack lets each target benchmark weight several imputers (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, nearest-neighbour models, column median), with a median-shrinkage fallback when the stack does not beat the median in cross-validation.\nAll tuning uses history models only.\n\n\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n\\textbf{Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation).} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n\n\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n\\paragraph{Representation and EM.} Two representations of the history are built: z-scoring (clipped at $\\pm8$) and an empirical rank-Gauss map $\\Phi^{-1}\\big((r-\\tfrac12)/n\\big)$ per benchmark (average ranks; benchmarks with fewer than eight observations or four distinct values fall back to z-scores). For each, EM fits $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ with missing entries, applying diagonal shrinkage $\\Sigma\\leftarrow(1-\\epsilon)\\Sigma+\\epsilon\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Sigma)$ ($\\epsilon=0.02$) in each M-step (at most 60 iterations, tolerance $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget).\n\n\\paragraph{Selection.} Candidate subsets come from (i) covariance-trace greedy A-optimal selection: with $R$ the current posterior covariance, add $j$ maximising $\\sum_{i\\in U,i\\ne j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$, then update $R\\leftarrow R-R_{\\cdot j}R_{j\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\lambda)$; and (ii) a greedy forward \\emph{wrapper} that adds the benchmark minimising five-fold held-out-model squared error of the conditional-Gaussian predictor, with all candidates scored by batched matrix products and an optional coverage weighting (benchmarks missing for many history models are less informative). For each representation and candidate rule, the subset with the lowest history cross-validation error in raw benchmark units is chosen (up to 250 candidates per step).\n\n\\paragraph{Imputation.} For a new model, the Gaussian conditional mean $\\mu+\\Sigma_{\\cdot S}(\\Sigma_{SS}+\\lambda I)^{-1}(y_S-\\mu_S)$ conditions only on the selected benchmarks that model actually has. Shrinkage, factor rank and $\\lambda$ are tuned by five-fold history cross-validation. The stack uses, per target benchmark, non-negative weights over six base predictors (three Gaussian conditional means at shrinkage 0, 0.15 and 0.3, ridge on the selected benchmarks, and kNN with 5 and 15 neighbours) plus an implicit column-median term, fitted on out-of-fold history predictions and regularised toward a pooled weight vector; a regularisation strength is chosen from six values by cross-validation. Outputs are mapped back to raw units, clipped to the history range, and replaced by the observed value for selected benchmarks; a model with no selected score receives the column median.\n\n\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds per seed. The method presumes a history matrix with enough coverage per benchmark and a new model whose scores are exchangeable with history models after transformation, which family-disjoint evaluation tests but does not guarantee. Some stages are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\n\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation.} Within a leaderboard, values are averaged over seeds, then over its $k$ grid with equal weights; the pooled metric averages the three leaderboards with weight $1/3$ each. The permuted-control gap is the control $\\nrmse$ minus the matched real-episode $\\nrmse$, averaged over seeds and then over leaderboards. A run counts as valid only if all episodes complete with finite predictions, real episodes beat their controls, and pooled skill against column mean and random-$k$ ridge is positive. The sealed evaluation finished with outcome \\emph{qualified}. This statement concerns the evaluated setting; no further qualification diagnostics were retained.\n\n\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_results.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Main results}\nPooled over the three leaderboards, the proposed pair reaches $\\nrmse=0.562$ and a skill of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, that is a 43.4\\% reduction in relative error (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]]\nThe three per-seed pooled skills are 0.433, 0.427 and 0.433, so the stochasticity of the method's own cross-validation folds moves the headline by less than 0.006. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\nThis supports H1 in the evaluated setting. Because all three seeds use identical held-out models, this spread does not measure how the result would change with different held-out models, and we report no confidence interval for the pooled values.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics of the frozen pair (mean over seeds 201--203; hierarchy described in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}). Lower $\\nrmse$ is better; skill is relative error reduction against the named baseline.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\n\\midrule\nPooled $\\nrmse$ & 0.562 & mean over 3 leaderboards\\\\\n\\quad MTEB / HELM Lite / Open LLM LB & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per-leaderboard $\\nrmse$\\\\\n\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787 / 0.722 / 0.664 / 0.638 & error vs.\\ budget\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ column mean & 0.434 & H1\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ greedy-forward ridge & 0.099 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nSkill vs.\\ random-$k$ ridge & 0.016 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\nPermuted-control gap (nRMSE) & 0.678 & control minus real\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_helm]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_oll]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\nOn MTEB, $\\nrmse$ falls monotonically from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.722 at $k{=}5$, 0.664 at $k{=}10$ and 0.638 at $k{=}20$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}a). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k2]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k5]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k10]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20]]\nThe mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n\\paragraph{Control.} The permuted-observation control raises $\\nrmse$ by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes (Figure~\\ref{tab:final} lists the value). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_permuted_control_gap]]\nHence the predictions depend on the new model's own scores on the selected benchmarks rather than on a marginal prior alone. The gap is an absolute $\\nrmse$ difference and should be read against the pooled error of 0.562.\n\n\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has about 1.6\\% lower relative error than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\nBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n\n\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n\n\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\nDuring development, three configurations were scored on a separate MTEB partition (disjoint groups, 280 columns, 131 history and 32 held-out models) with two seeds and $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$. Table~\\ref{tab:search} keeps these numbers apart from the sealed ones; $\\nrmse$ values are not comparable across the two partitions.\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition comparison (MTEB, two seeds). Score is the mean skill against column mean. The seed columns give the two per-seed scores.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11 / 12 & $\\nrmse$ & Skill vs.\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\ gap\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair (trace greedy + conditional mean) & 0.220 & 0.220 / 0.220 & 1.067 & 0.100 & 0.522\\\\\nProposed pair (wrapper + stack) & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\nProposed + latent-skill member & 0.247 & 0.243 / 0.251 & 1.028 & 0.132 & 0.568\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on the best-scoring configuration; the exact tie means the retained scores alone cannot distinguish the two sources (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with 43.4\\% lower pooled relative error than column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within 1.6\\% of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked pair on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562, a 43.4\\% relative reduction against column means, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\nGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Columns & History models & Held-out models & Groups (hist./held-out) & $k$ grid\\\\\n\\midrule\nMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\nOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\nHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (\\texttt{src/program.py}, functions \\texttt{em\\_gaussian\\_covariance}, \\texttt{greedy\\_select\\_conditional\\_variance}, \\texttt{wrapper\\_forward\\_select\\_cv}, \\texttt{stack\\_predictors\\_nnls}, \\texttt{predict\\_raw}). % @evidence[[results/ideas/02_wrapper_cv_error_benchmark_selection_with_robust_quantile_transformed_stacked_imputation/program_1/src/program.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair.}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\n\\midrule\nEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\nCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\nCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\nFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\nRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\nSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\nWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\nStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\nStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\nClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\nWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\nThe latent code is therefore executed but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated program tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The description in the main text is of the shared stack, and the extension is described here for completeness. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n\n\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\nProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\nProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\nThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\nEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used for research ideation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their records.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
