{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw9zH5T2rwvvfomVV2Ej","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LQmyE7vd3Bcq8iGLhzvmRe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b,cnt=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==cnt,(s.count(a),a[:50])\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\n\n# 1. derivation in design rationale\nrep(\"\"\"We state this only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment,\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Concretely, for a row-vector of scores $y\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mu,\\\\Sigma)$ and observation noise $\\\\lambda\\\\ge 0$, the posterior covariance after observing benchmark $j$ is $R'=R-R_{\\\\cdot j}R_{j\\\\cdot}/(R_{jj}+\\\\lambda)$ with $R$ initialised at $\\\\Sigma$, and the resulting drop of the summed variance over the remaining benchmarks is $\\\\sum_{i\\\\in U,\\\\,i\\\\neq j}R_{ij}^2/(R_{jj}+\\\\lambda)$. A benchmark scores highly if it is strongly correlated with many others and itself has substantial variance, so redundant benchmarks are avoided once one of them has been chosen, because the update deflates their shared variance. The classical near-optimality guarantees for greedy selection \\\\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor} apply to submodular objectives; we do not claim that the trace criterion with noise and missing data satisfies the required conditions here. We state the Gaussian argument only as motivation: we did not check it against a controlled experiment,\"\"\")\n\n# 2. validity of the protocol\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Aggregation.}\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Why this protocol answers the question.} Four design decisions tie the measurement to the research question. (i) Family-disjoint held-out models make the new model genuinely new: if siblings of a held-out model were in the history, a nearest-neighbour effect could masquerade as cross-benchmark structure. (ii) Committing to $S$ before any new-model score is observed matches the budgeted setting in which the evaluator cannot adapt the run list, and it prevents the selector from peeking at the targets. (iii) Equal weight per held-out model, and errors in units of each benchmark's history spread, stop benchmarks with large numeric ranges or models with many observed cells from dominating the metric. (iv) Evaluator-owned baselines and permutation controls keep the comparison objects outside the control of the method: the method cannot tune its own competitors. The remaining weakness is that the held-out set is fixed, which we return to in Section~\\\\ref{sec:limits}.\n\n\\\\paragraph{Aggregation.}\"\"\")\n\n# 3. new results subsection before configuration comparison\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\\\label{sec:search}\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\subsection{Reading the metrics: sensitivity and failure analysis}\n\\\\paragraph{Skill is a mean of ratios.} Each skill value is computed per episode and then averaged along the hierarchy of Section~\\\\ref{sec:protocol}, and is clipped to $[-1,1]$. It is therefore not equal to one minus the ratio of pooled errors. If it were, the two ridge-baseline skills would imply that the greedy-forward baseline has an error about $(1-0.016)/(1-0.099)\\\\approx 1.09$ times that of the random-$k$ baseline. We read this only as indicative of the sign and rough size of the effect. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n\\\\paragraph{Where the pair could fail.} Three mechanisms can degrade the predictions without being visible in the pooled numbers: (a) benchmarks with few history observations fall back to z-scores or to coarse medians, and the clipped outputs cannot extrapolate beyond the history range, so a new model that outperforms every history model on a benchmark is necessarily under-predicted on that benchmark; (b) selected benchmarks that a new model has not been run on contribute nothing, and the pair then falls back to a prior that conditions on fewer scores (a model with none of the selected scores receives column medians); (c) the wall-clock guards switch off the stack or optional candidates on slow hardware. We did not construct stress tests for (a)--(c), so we cannot report how often or by how much they matter.\n\\\\paragraph{What a different outcome would have looked like.} A permuted-control gap near zero would have meant that the predictions were driven by the marginal prior; a negative skill against column means would have refuted H1; a flat MTEB curve would have refuted H2. The first two did not occur and the third is contradicted by the monotone curve, so the study could have refuted them. By contrast, H3 and H4 are not tested sharply: the sealed metrics do not isolate selection, and the leaderboards are not matched in size.\n\n\\\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\\\label{sec:search}\"\"\")\n\n# 4. discussion additions\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Practical reading.}\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\\\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\\\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\\\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\\\emph{Configuration freezing.} The pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\\\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same ideas could behave differently.\n\n\\\\paragraph{Practical reading.}\"\"\")\n# tables fixes\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\\\\\"\"\",\"\"\"\\\\small\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Meaning\\\\\\\\\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\quad MTEB / HELM Lite / Open LLM LB & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per-leaderboard $\\\\nrmse$\\\\\\\\\n\\\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787 / 0.722 / 0.664 / 0.638 & error vs.\\\\ budget\\\\\\\\\"\"\",\"\"\"\\\\quad MTEB / HELM / Open LLM & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per leaderboard\\\\\\\\\n\\\\quad MTEB $k{=}2/5/10/20$ & 0.787/0.722/0.664/0.638 & error vs.\\\\ budget\\\\\\\\\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11 / 12 & $\\\\nrmse$ & Skill vs.\\\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\\\ gap\\\\\\\\\"\"\",\"\"\"\\\\small\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Seeds 11/12 & $\\\\nrmse$ & vs.\\\\ random-$k$ & Perm.\\\\ gap\\\\\\\\\"\"\")\nrep(\"Simple pair (trace greedy + conditional mean)\",\"Simple pair (trace + cond.\\\\ mean)\")\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Columns & History models & Held-out models & Groups (hist./held-out) & $k$ grid\\\\\\\\\"\"\",\"\"\"\\\\small\n\\\\setlength{\\\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Cols. & History & Held-out & Groups (h/o) & $k$ grid\\\\\\\\\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\\\\\"\"\",\"\"\"\\\\small\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lp{0.62\\\\textwidth}}\n\\\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\\\\\